</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Kai Shu
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Emory University
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_email"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Email: </span><a href="mailto:"><span id="id4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">kai.shu@emory.edu</span></a>
</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Large Language Models (LLMs) often struggle with producing factually consistent answers due to limitations in their parametric memory. Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) methods address this issue by incorporating external knowledge from trusted sources at inference time. However, such methods typically treat knowledge as unstructured text, which limits their ability to support compositional reasoning and identify factual inconsistencies.
To overcome these limitations, we propose a novel framework that dynamically constructs and expands knowledge graphs (KGs) during inference, integrating both internal knowledge extracted from LLMs and external information retrieved from external sources. Our method begins by extracting a seed KG from the question via prompting, followed by iterative expansion using the LLM’s latent knowledge. The graph is then selectively refined through external retrieval, enhancing factual coverage and correcting inaccuracies.
We evaluate our approach on three diverse factual QA benchmarks, demonstrating consistent improvements in factual accuracy, answer precision, and interpretability over baseline prompting and static KG-augmented methods. Our findings suggest that inference-time KG construction is a promising direction for enhancing LLM factuality in a structured, interpretable, and scalable manner. The code for this paper is available at <a href="https://github.com/ShanglinWu/autokg.git" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter">https://github.com/ShanglinWu/autokg.git</a>.</p>
  
</div>
<div id="p1" class="ltx_para ltx_align_bottom">
<p id="p1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center" style="width:345.0pt;"><span id="p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_inline-block" style="width:0.0pt;"></span></p>
</div>
<figure id="S0.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.03540v1/0.png" id="S0.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_portrait" style="aspect-ratio:429/540;" width="429" height="540" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span>
Comparison of three methods for answering factual questions:
(a) Chain-of-Thought prompting,
(b) Answering with a KG constructed from the LLM’s internal knowledge,
(c) Answering with a KG grounded in external knowledge.
Grounding with external knowledge corrects incorrect entities and expands the graph with new edges, ultimately leading to the correct answer.
In this example, both (a) and (b) focus on an image from Greek mythology, which is incorrect, highlighting the limitation of relying solely on the LLM’s internal knowledge base.
Details of the KG construction process are provided in Section <a href="#S3" title="3 Method ‣ Improving Factuality in LLMs via Inference-Time Knowledge Graph Construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>.
</figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Large Language Models (LLMs) have demonstrated remarkable capabilities in natural language understanding <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Karanikolas et al. (2023)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kumar (2024)</a></cite>, content generation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Moore et al. (2023)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nejjar et al. (2023)</a></cite>, and reasoning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Saparov and He (2022)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Feng et al. (2023)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hao et al. (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">An et al. (2023)</a></cite>. However, propensity to generate content inconsistent with factual information, presents substantial risks in high-stakes domains and degrades their performance <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al. (2024b)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al. (2024a)</a></cite>. This issue have been highlighted by evaluating LLMs on factual question answering task, where LLMs are required to provide accurate answers to questions based on verifiable facts. Resent research indicates that even for relatively straightforward factual retrieval tasks, LLMs struggle with reliably grounding their responses <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang et al. (2025)</a></cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To this end, recent works reveal the effectiveness of merging structured data, especially knowledge graphs (KGs) at inference time to enhance LLMs’ factuality <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Pan et al. (2024)</a></cite><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yao et al. (2023)</a></cite><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sun et al. (2023)</a></cite>. KGs represent facts as interconnected triples of entities and relations, offering explicit structure that supports compositional reasoning, and mitigates hallucination. Instead of relying purely on unstructured parametric memory, reasoning over structured KG enables LLMs to access and combine factual knowledge systematically. The graph-based representation of knowledge facilitates the identification of knowledge deficiencies<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zheng et al. (2023)</a></cite>, provides the potential to expand and refine the knowledge graph precisely during inference time, enabling more accurate answers to factual questions.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Many existing methods rely on pre-constructed knowledge graphs derived from static knowledge bases as their primary source of factual information <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sen et al. (2023)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Baek et al. (2023)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wu et al. (2023)</a></cite>. While these external sources are typically well-curated and offer domain-specific knowledge, constructing and maintaining them demands substantial human effort. This often limits scalability and adaptability, especially in fast-evolving knowledge contexts. In contrast, LLMs are trained on massive text corpora, which endow them with broad but implicit knowledge across diverse topics<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gekhman et al. (2025)</a></cite>. Although their internal knowledge bases may be less comprehensive or precise than curated external sources, they offer a key advantage: knowledge extraction from LLMs can be performed on-demand with minimal manual intervention, allowing for rapid, dynamic construction of knowledge graphs tailored to specific inference tasks. This contrast raises a natural question:
<span id="S1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Can we combine the strengths of both paradigms leveraging the customizability and reliability of external knowledge bases with the adaptability and efficiency of LLM-derived knowledge graphs?</span> Such a hybrid approach could enable real-time KG construction and expansion during inference-time, improving LLMs’ factual reasoning ability while minimizing human labor.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">To illustrate the core motivation and methodological differences, Figure <a href="#S0.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ Improving Factuality in LLMs via Inference-Time Knowledge Graph Construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> compares three approaches to factual question answering: (a) Chain-of-Thought (CoT) prompting; (b) answering with a KG constructed from the LLM’s internal knowledge; and (c) answering with a KG further refined through external retrieval. Compared to unstructured CoT reasoning, KGs make the reasoning process more explicit and interpretable. Moreover, incorporating external knowledge allows the model to revise missing or inaccurate triplets, thereby improving factual coverage and answer accuracy.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">In this paper, we address the challenge of improving the factual reliability of LLMs by leveraging structured knowledge representations. We propose a novel pipeline that constructs and expands knowledge graphs dynamically during inference-time, drawing from both internal LLM representations and external knowledge bases. The main contributions of our work are:</p>
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Inference-Time Knowledge Graph Construction:</span> We present a method to extract factual triplets directly from LLMs’ internal knowledge through prompting, design an inference-time expansion strategy that iteratively extends the graph with relevant entities and relations. Our method is guided by the model’s evolving reasoning needs, thereby enhancing coverage and contextual relevance.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Hybrid Knowledge Integration:</span> We explore a unified approach that combines internal LLM-derived knowledge with curated external knowledge bases via knowledge graphs. This hybrid setup enhances factual grounding by compensating for gaps in either source.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Empirical Validation on Factual QA Tasks:</span> We evaluate our approach on multiple factual question answering benchmarks, demonstrating consistent improvements in answer accuracy and factual consistency.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Related Work</h2>

<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1 </span>Construct KG by LLMs</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Recent advances in LLMs have led to growing interest in using them for knowledge graph construction. Traditional KG construction is a labor-intensive task requiring extensive domain expertise, whereas LLMs offer the potential to automate this process by leveraging their pre-trained knowledge and natural language understanding.  <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kommineni et al. (2024)</a></cite> introduce a semi-automatic pipeline where an LLM generates competency questions to guide ontology construction and triple extraction from academic texts.  <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nie et al. (2024)</a></cite> employ chain-of-thought prompting to improve relation extraction consistency using predefined schemas, while enhancing graph quality through ontology alignment.  <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xu et al. (2024)</a></cite> present Generate-on-Graph (GoG), a training-free method where an LLM dynamically augments incomplete KGs during question answering by injecting new triples based on its world knowledge. For data-scarce settings,  <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al. (2024)</a></cite> propose FKGC, which synthesizes plausible triples for rare relations and distills them into smaller models with consistency checks.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">LLM-based KG construction has also been explored in various domain-specific and QA-driven contexts.  <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bai et al. (2025)</a></cite> construct a 2-million-node KG (KG-FM) from over 100,000 research papers using Qwen2-72B, enabling accurate QA through KG-augmented inference. Meanwhile,  <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ding et al. (2024)</a></cite> propose pipelines for theme-specific and cybersecurity KGs, guided by domain-specific ontologies to enhance structure and accuracy. Complementing these text-based approaches,  <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cohen et al. (2023)</a></cite> propose LMCRAWL, a method for extracting knowledge graphs directly from the internal knowledge of LLMs. Starting from seed entities, the model is prompted to recursively generate related triples, building a structured KG without relying on external corpora. Their results show that LLMs encode a substantial amount of factual knowledge that can be surfaced with high precision. Together, these works reflect a broader shift toward LLM-centric, structure-aware pipelines that combine prompt design, schema constraints, and minimal supervision to extract and utilize structured knowledge for tasks such as question answering and KG completion.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2 </span>Knowledge Graphs for Factual QA</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Recent works have highlighted the effectiveness of KGs in enhancing factual question answering.  <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Pan et al. (2024)</a></cite> propose a roadmap for unifying LLMs and KGs, arguing that structured, verified facts provided by KGs help mitigate hallucinations and ensure factual consistency. They demonstrate that retrieving from KGs supplements the parametric memory of LLMs and supports more accurate factual retrieval.  <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yao et al. (2023)</a></cite> and  <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sun et al. (2023)</a></cite> emphasize that the explicit graph structure enables compositional, multi-hop reasoning by linking related facts through traversable paths. Their studies show that reasoning over KGs naturally facilitates complex inference steps that are difficult for LLMs relying solely on free-text generation. Empirically,  <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ma et al. (2025)</a></cite> introduce Debate-on-Graph (DoG), a KG-augmented reasoning framework, achieving a 23.7% improvement in accuracy over prior methods on WebQuestions. Their results highlight that incorporating KG-based reasoning loops leads to more systematic, interpretable, and fact-grounded QA performance.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.3 </span>Factual Question Answering</h3>

<div id="S2.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Factual question answering has been widely explored across different data sources and reasoning formats. Despite recent breakthroughs in LLMs, state-of-the-art models still face notable limitations in producing consistent and verifiable factual responses across QA benchmarks. These shortcomings have renewed interest in structured approaches such as knowledge graph-based QA to improve factual accuracy and reasoning transparency. Factual QA benchmarks are generally classified into three categories according to knowledge source: <span id="S2.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Knowledge Base QA (KBQA)</span> focuses on answering questions from curated knowledge bases such as Freebase and Wikidata. Datasets like SimpleQuestions <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bordes et al. (2015)</a></cite> and WebQuestionsSP <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yih et al. (2016)</a></cite> provide natural language questions mapped to single-hop and multi-hop facts, while ComplexWebQuestions <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Talmor and Berant (2018)</a></cite> and LC-QuAD 2.0 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dubey et al. (2019)</a></cite> introduce complex logical, comparative, and numerical queries. <span id="S2.SS3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Document-based QA</span> requires answering questions using evidence from unstructured text. HotpotQA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al. (2018)</a></cite> is a widely used multi-hop benchmark where models must synthesize information across multiple paragraphs, while ConditionalQA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sun and et al. (2022)</a></cite> includes questions whose answers depend on specific conditions mentioned in context. DROP <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dua et al. (2019)</a></cite> poses challenges involving numerical, comparative, and logical reasoning. <span id="S2.SS3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Expert-curated QA</span> datasets, such as SimpleQA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wei (2024)</a></cite>, are designed to assess factual precision under adversarial and high-accuracy settings. SimpleQA consists of simple fact-seeking questions with verifiable answers, carefully curated by human annotators and adversarially tested against GPT-4.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Method</h2>

<figure id="S3.F2" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.03540v1/1.png" id="S3.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:713/577;" width="713" height="577" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2: </span>Overview of our pipeline. The process begins with <span id="S3.F2.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(A) Graph initialization</span>, in which the input question is parsed by the LLM to extract initial triplets. Next, <span id="S3.F2.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(B) Graph expansion</span> iteratively explores breadth<span id="S3.F2.9" class="ltx_text">-</span>first relations from seed entities to build a larger KG. Following this, in <span id="S3.F2.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(D) External retrieval</span>, search is performed (e.g., using BM25 on the content returned from wikipedia and google search) to correct or extend selected triplets, which are merged into the graph. Finally, <span id="S3.F2.11" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(E)Answering on Graph</span>, a refined KG supports factual answer generation, yielding a grounded response.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Existing KG-based QA methods typically rely on either (i) manually curated knowledge bases, or (ii) extraction of entities and relations only from the LLM’s parametric knowledge.
In contrast, our approach introduces two novel components:
(1) an automated pipeline that guides the LLM to iteratively construct and expand the knowledge graph from the input question without requiring predefined schemas, and
(2) an external grounding mechanism that integrates information from open-domain retrieval (Wikipedia, Google Search) to correct or expand incomplete or incorrect triplets. Formally, a Knowledge Graph (KG) is represented as <math id="S3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G=(s,r,o)\subseteq E\times R\times E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>o</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G=(s,r,o)\subseteq E\times R\times E</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the set of entities and <math id="S3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> represents the set of relations. Each triplet <math id="S3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(s,r,o)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>o</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(s,r,o)</annotation></semantics></math> consists of a subject-relation-object structure, where <math id="S3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s,o\in E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>o</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s,o\in E</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\in R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\in R</annotation></semantics></math>. Additionally, we use <math id="S3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math> to represent a given question, and <math id="S3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="LM" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">LM</annotation></semantics></math> denote a language model.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">As illustrated in Figure <a href="#S3.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 3 Method ‣ Improving Factuality in LLMs via Inference-Time Knowledge Graph Construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, the process can be formally described as follows: <span id="S3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Graph Initialization:</span> The process begins by initializing a graph <math id="S3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> from the given question <math id="S3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q\rightarrow LM\rightarrow G_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q\rightarrow LM\rightarrow G_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, which includes the entities and relations mentioned within the question.
<span id="S3.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Graph Expansion:</span> In this step, the graph <math id="S3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> is expanded in a breadth-first order. For each iteration, the LLM generates a new relation <math id="S3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> and object <math id="S3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="o" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>o</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">o</annotation></semantics></math> from a selected source entity <math id="S3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math>. This expansion continues until the graph reaches a depth of <math id="S3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math>, resulting in the expanded graph <math id="S3.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>.
<span id="S3.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">External Retrieval:</span> When leveraging the knowledge graph to answer questions, the LLM may encounter incorrect or incomplete triplets, either containing factually incorrect entities (incorrect) or lacking sufficient detail and thus requiring further expansion to reach the correct answer (incomplete). To address this, the LLM modifies or expands the graph <math id="S3.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, such as replacing an erroneous triplet <math id="S3.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(s,r,o)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>o</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(s,r,o)</annotation></semantics></math> with a corrected triplet <math id="S3.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(s,r^{\prime},o^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>r</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>o</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(s,r^{\prime},o^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math>. During this stage, the LLM has access to external knowledge sources. The revised graph, incorporating external information, is denoted as <math id="S3.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>.
<span id="S3.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Answering on the Graph:</span> The final step involves the LLM generating an answer to the question <math id="S3.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math> by querying the relations and entities within the graph <math id="S3.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Graph Initialization</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Given a question <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math>, the LLM is first prompted to extract relevant entities and relations. Specifically, the model is instructed: <span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">"Extract meaningful entities and relations from the given question in order to answer it."</span> The LLM then returns a set of triplets that capture the core focus and informational intent of <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math>. These triplets are utilized to initialize the knowledge graph <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, which forms the basis for the subsequent expansion process.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Graph Expansion</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This stage forms the core mechanism for constructing a KG from the LLM’s internal knowledge. We design an iterative process in which the LLM selects a subset of entities deemed valuable for further exploration. These selected entities, denoted as <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{s}</annotation></semantics></math>, are placed into a buffer <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>. The LLM then proceeds to explore each entity in <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> using a breadth-first strategy. During each iteration, the LLM generates one new triplet related to the current entity. This process continues until the buffer is empty or a predefined depth <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> limit is reached. The resulting expanded graph is denoted as <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>. The corresponding pseudocode is provided in Algorithm <a href="#algorithm1" title="In 3.2 Graph Expansion ‣ 3 Method ‣ Improving Factuality in LLMs via Inference-Time Knowledge Graph Construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="algorithm1" class="ltx_float ltx_algorithm ltx_framed ltx_framed_top">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="algorithm1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 1</span> </span>BuildKnowledgeGraph</figcaption>
<div id="algorithm1.4" class="ltx_listing ltx_lst_numbers_left ltx_framed ltx_framed_topbottom ltx_listing">
<div id="algorithm1.4.1" class="ltx_listingline">
<span id="algorithm1.4.1.1" class="ltx_text"><span id="algorithm1.4.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Input:</span> </span>Question <math id="algorithm1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>, max depth <math id="algorithm1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.4.2" class="ltx_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.4.2.1" class="ltx_text"><span id="algorithm1.4.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Output:</span> </span>Knowledge Graph <math id="algorithm1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.4.3" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.4.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">1</span></span>
            Initialize graph <math id="algorithm1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, visited set <math id="algorithm1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math>, queue <math id="algorithm1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math>, buffer <math id="algorithm1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> 
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.4.4" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.4.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">2</span></span>
            Extract initial entities <math id="algorithm1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> and relations <math id="algorithm1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> from <math id="algorithm1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> 
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.4.5" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.4.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">3</span></span>
            Add <math id="algorithm1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="algorithm1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> into <math id="algorithm1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>; <math id="algorithm1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V\leftarrow E_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V\leftarrow E_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> 
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.4.6" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.4.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">4</span></span>
            <math id="algorithm1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q\leftarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q\leftarrow</annotation></semantics></math> Filtered entities from <math id="algorithm1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>

