<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 1: </span>Performance comparison across three QA tasks: CWQ (Knowledge Base QA), HotpotQA (Document QA), and SimpleQA (Expert-curated QA). EM and Accuracy are reported separately for CWQ and HotpotQA. Accuracy is reported for SimpleQA.</figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Datasets</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The test questions should be simple and concise, with answers consisting of a single entity. This ensures that the module can accurately interpret the question, allowing the evaluation to focus on factual grounding. Considering the diversity of knowledge sources and the broad coverage of topics and entities, we select three representative datasets from Section <a href="#S2.SS3" title="2.3 Factual Question Answering ‣ 2 Related Work ‣ Improving Factuality in LLMs via Inference-Time Knowledge Graph Construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.3</span></a>, encompassing Knowledge Base Question Answering (KBQA), Document-based QA, and Expert-curated QA. <span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Complex WebQuestions(CWQ)<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Talmor and Berant (2018)</a></cite></span> is a dataset designed to evaluate the ability of question answering systems to decompose complex questions into simpler sub-questions and provide answers. It contains 34,689 complex questions, each paired with a SPARQL query executable against Freebase. <span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">HotpotQA<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al. (2018)</a></cite></span> is a large-scale dataset consisting of 113,000 crowd-sourced questions, each requiring reasoning over multiple supporting documents from the English Wikipedia. All questions have sentence-level supporting facts provided. <span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">SimpleQA<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wei (2024)</a></cite></span> is a benchmark comprising 4,326 short, fact-seeking questions. Each question is designed to have a single, indisputable answer, and responses are graded as correct, incorrect, or not attempted. The dataset thus serves as a concise yet rigorous testbed for evaluating LLMs’ factual precision in QA systems.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>Experiment Settings</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In our experiments, we evaluate the performance of various models on CWQ-test, (3,519 questions), HotpotQA-dev(7,405 questions), and SimpleQA (4,326 questions). For HotpotQA-dev, we only provide questions to LLM without the support contexts. The evaluation metrics include Exact Match (EM) and LLM-assisted judge accuracy (Acc) on HotpotQA and CWQ, for SimpleQA we utilize simple-evals tools. For the LLM-assisted judge, we utilize GPT-4o-mini. The models under evaluation are GPT-4o, Deepseek-v3, Gemini-2.5-flash, Qwen2.5-32B-instruct, and Llama-4-scout. For external retrieval, we utilize Serper API for google search and wikipedia python package. The results of these evaluations are presented in Table <a href="#S4.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Improving Factuality in LLMs via Inference-Time Knowledge Graph Construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>. Detailed prompt configurations are provided in Appendix <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Prompts ‣ Improving Factuality in LLMs via Inference-Time Knowledge Graph Construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>. More detailed experimental settings can be found in Appendix <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Hyperparameters ‣ Improving Factuality in LLMs via Inference-Time Knowledge Graph Construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.3 </span>Main Results</h3>

<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#S4.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Improving Factuality in LLMs via Inference-Time Knowledge Graph Construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> presents a comprehensive evaluation of five LLMs across the selected datasets under three different settings: (a) Chain-of-Thought prompting (CoT), (b) knowledge graph construction using internal model knowledge only (KG w. Internal), and (c) knowledge graph augmentation with external retrieval (KG w. External). These three settings reflect increasing levels of structure and factual grounding, progressing from purely unstructured reasoning to explicitly constructed and verifiable knowledge representations. Across all models and datasets, we observe a consistent and substantial improvement in performance when external retrieval is incorporated into the knowledge graph construction. This highlights the robustness and generality of our proposed approach. Specifically, our method yields significant gains over the standard CoT prompting baseline. For instance, Deepseek-V3 achieves a 12% absolute improvement in exact match on CWQ, and Llama-4-scout sees a remarkable 25.4% increase in accuracy on SimpleQA. These results are particularly notable given the diverse nature of the datasets.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">However, the KG w. Internal Knowledge setting — where knowledge graphs are constructed solely from the LLM’s internal generations — does not consistently improve model performance. In some cases, it even leads to degradation. For example, GPT-4o achieves lower accuracy on SimpleQA under this setting compared to CoT prompting. This observation suggests that while constructing KGs from internal knowledge can help elicit factual content, the process is not robust enough to ensure consistent gains. Moreover, performance disparities are especially pronounced on SimpleQA. Notably, Qwen2.5-32B and LLama-4-scout both achieve under 10% accuracy when relying solely on internal knowledge. This underscores a critical limitation: although LLMs encode a vast amount of implicit factual knowledge, such knowledge is often incomplete, imprecise, or difficult to extract reliably.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">By using knowledge graphs as a mediator between internal representations and external knowledge sources, our method raises the performance of smaller models to be on par with much larger ones. For instance, Llama-4-scout, when equipped with external KG retrieval, even surpasses both Deepseek-V3 and Gemini-2.5-flash on SimpleQA — despite having significantly fewer parameters. This demonstrates the potential of external KG augmentation to bridge the capability gap across LLMs of varying scales and settings.</p>
</div>
