<td id="S4.T2.2.16.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="--ltx-bg-color:#E6E6FF;padding-top:0.5pt;padding-bottom:0.5pt;"><span id="S4.T2.2.16.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-bg-color:#E6E6FF;">54.0</span></td>
<td id="S4.T2.2.16.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="--ltx-bg-color:#E6E6FF;padding-top:0.5pt;padding-bottom:0.5pt;"><span id="S4.T2.2.16.3.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-bg-color:#E6E6FF;">48.6</span></td>
<td id="S4.T2.2.16.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="--ltx-bg-color:#E6E6FF;padding-top:0.5pt;padding-bottom:0.5pt;"><span id="S4.T2.2.16.4.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-bg-color:#E6E6FF;">41.2</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T2.2.17" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T2.2.17.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row" style="padding-top:0.5pt;padding-bottom:0.5pt;">   <math id="S4.T2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<td id="S4.T2.2.17.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding-top:0.5pt;padding-bottom:0.5pt;">+4.7</td>
<td id="S4.T2.2.17.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding-top:0.5pt;padding-bottom:0.5pt;">+9.0</td>
<td id="S4.T2.2.17.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding-top:0.5pt;padding-bottom:0.5pt;">+31.9</td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T2.2.18" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T2.2.18.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:0.5pt;padding-bottom:0.5pt;" colspan="4"><span id="S4.T2.2.18.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Deepseek-V3</span></th></tr>
<tr id="S4.T2.2.19" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T2.2.19.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row" style="padding-top:0.5pt;padding-bottom:0.5pt;">   Internal KG</th>
<td id="S4.T2.2.19.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding-top:0.5pt;padding-bottom:0.5pt;">53.9</td>
<td id="S4.T2.2.19.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding-top:0.5pt;padding-bottom:0.5pt;">49.2</td>
<td id="S4.T2.2.19.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding-top:0.5pt;padding-bottom:0.5pt;">24.3</td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T2.2.20" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T2.2.20.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row" style="padding-top:0.5pt;padding-bottom:0.5pt;">   + External</th>
<td id="S4.T2.2.20.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="--ltx-bg-color:#E6E6FF;padding-top:0.5pt;padding-bottom:0.5pt;"><span id="S4.T2.2.20.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-bg-color:#E6E6FF;">57.1</span></td>
<td id="S4.T2.2.20.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="--ltx-bg-color:#E6E6FF;padding-top:0.5pt;padding-bottom:0.5pt;"><span id="S4.T2.2.20.3.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-bg-color:#E6E6FF;">54.3</span></td>
<td id="S4.T2.2.20.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="--ltx-bg-color:#E6E6FF;padding-top:0.5pt;padding-bottom:0.5pt;"><span id="S4.T2.2.20.4.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-bg-color:#E6E6FF;">47.0</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T2.2.21" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T2.2.21.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_bb" style="padding-top:0.5pt;padding-bottom:0.5pt;">   <math id="S4.T2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<td id="S4.T2.2.21.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb" style="padding-top:0.5pt;padding-bottom:0.5pt;">+3.2</td>
<td id="S4.T2.2.21.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb" style="padding-top:0.5pt;padding-bottom:0.5pt;">+5.1</td>
<td id="S4.T2.2.21.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb" style="padding-top:0.5pt;padding-bottom:0.5pt;">+22.7</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 2: </span>Recall (%) of knowledge graphs constructed from internal LLM knowledge versus those enhanced with external retrieval across CWQ, HotpotQA, and SimpleQA. The bottom row of each model shows the absolute gain from external retrieval.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Limitations of Internal Knowledge Alone.</span>
To better understand the contribution of external retrieval in improving knowledge coverage of knowledge graphs, we compute the recall of the constructed graphs—i.e., the proportion of groundtruth appear in the graphs. The results are shown in Table <a href="#S4.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 4.3 Main Results ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Improving Factuality in LLMs via Inference-Time Knowledge Graph Construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>. Across all datasets and models, the recall consistently improves when external sources are integrated into the graph construction pipeline. For instance, on the SimpleQA dataset, Llama-4-scout shows a substantial recall increase from 9.3% to 41.2%, a gain of +31.9 points. Similarly, Qwen2.5-32B improves by +28.8 points on SimpleQA. These gains are particularly prominent on SimpleQA, a dataset with sparse but precise fact requirements, which highlights the brittleness of relying solely on internal LLM knowledge. Even for stronger models like GPT-4o and Deepseek-V3, external retrieval leads to consistent improvements across CWQ and HotpotQA. These findings highlight the limitations of relying solely on internal LLM knowledge and demonstrate the substantial benefits brought by external retrieval. The structured nature of knowledge graphs allows external retrieval to operate at the granularity of individual triplets, making the retrieval process more fine-grained and controllable. This design enables the system to achieve significant performance gains with minimal retrieval overhead, offering an efficient and scalable pathway to enhance factual coverage in LLM-based QA.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F3" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.03540v1/3.png" id="S4.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:605/165;" width="605" height="165" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 3: </span>Accuracy and graph size for five models across different hop counts. Solid lines represent accuracy, while dashed lines indicate graph size.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.4 </span>Ablation Study</h3>

<div id="S4.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Effect of number of hops</span>
HotpotQA is a multi-hop factual QA dataset in which each question is annotated with the number of reasoning hops required to arrive at the correct answer. To further investigate how this reasoning complexity impacts performance, we analyze the KG w. External method by examining both LLM-judged answer accuracy and the size of the constructed knowledge graphs, measured by the number of triplets per graph. As shown in Figure <a href="#S4.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 4.3 Main Results ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Improving Factuality in LLMs via Inference-Time Knowledge Graph Construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, we observe a clear trend: as the number of hops increases, the average graph size grows while the accuracy of the model’s answers declines.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">This behavior suggests that more complex questions, which require multi-hop reasoning across multiple facts, lead to generate larger initial graphs in an attempt to capture all potentially relevant entities and relations. These larger initial graphs, when further expanded and contribute to the overall increase in graph size. However, the expanded graph introduces more candidate paths and entities, which makes it increasingly difficult for the model to identify the correct answer node with high precision. The decline in accuracy reflects this challenge, revealing a trade-off between broader coverage through larger graphs and the precision required for accurate factual retrieval.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS4.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Effect of model scale</span>
We further investigate the relationship between model size, accuracy, and graph recall, as shown in Figure <a href="#S4.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 4.4 Ablation Study ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Improving Factuality in LLMs via Inference-Time Knowledge Graph Construction" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>. We select three models with varying sizes: Llama-4-scout (17B), Qwen2.5 (32B), and Deepseek-v3 (671B). Deepseek-v3 achieves the highest accuracy and graph recall across all hop levels, demonstrating the advantage of a larger internal knowledge base. Notably, it also exhibits the largest gap between accuracy and recall, indicating that, given a fixed amount of information in the graph, Deepseek-v3 is more effective at activating relevant internal knowledge. In contrast, Qwen2.5 performs the weakest, and as the hop count increases, its graph recall even surpasses its accuracy, suggesting a high sensitivity to reasoning difficulty and a relatively weak ability to leverage graph structure. Llama-4-scout shows greater robustness to hop complexity increase, indicating stronger capability in retrieving useful information from the graph in multi-hop scenarios.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F4" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.03540v1/4.png" id="S4.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_portrait" style="aspect-ratio:184/274;" width="184" height="274" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 4: </span>Accuracy and graph recall across different hop counts. Solid lines represent accuracy, while dashed lines indicate graph recall.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Conclusion</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this paper, we present a pipeline for inference-time knowledge graph construction that integrates LLM-derived and external knowledge. This approach improves factual accuracy, interpretability, and robustness in QA, with experiments showing its effectiveness in bridging knowledge gaps and supporting multi-hop reasoning.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6 </span>Limitations</h2>

<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Several limitations remain for our work: (a) When constructing knowledge graphs from the LLM’s internal knowledge base, there is a risk of generating hallucinated content. Such noise can propagate through the graph and lead to incorrect answers. (b) A noticeable gap persists between graph recall and exact match performance, indicating that current graph retrieval mechanisms are still suboptimal. Improving the accuracy and relevance of graph construction and retrieval may further enhance overall performance.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S7" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">7 </span>Ethical Consideration</h2>

<div id="S7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our work focuses on enhancing factual grounding in LLM-based QA systems.
Potential benefits include improved trustworthiness in applications like education, healthcare, and legal domains. However, misapplication might lead to overreliance on automated answers or misuse in misinformation contexts.
We mitigate these risks by grounding responses in verifiable external sources and making reasoning chains interpretable. We also ensure that any dataset used complies with licensing and privacy regulations.
Finally, all datasets used in our study are publicly released for academic research. HotpotQA is distributed under Apache License 2.0, Complex WebQuestions (CWQ) is released under the Creative Commons Attribution-ShareAlike (CC-BY-SA) license, and SimpleQA is distributed under the MIT License. We do not collect or redistribute any data from private or user-generated sources. Any content retrieved externally (e.g., via Google Search or Wikipedia) is used only at inference time for model augmentation and is not retained, repackaged, or released. Such use aligns with fair use principles for academic research.</p>
</div>
</section>
